    "marks": 9,
    "model": "From part (i): 1 mol of hydrocarbon uses 2.5 mol O₂ and produces 1 mol H₂O.\nLet the hydrocarbon be CₓHᵧ:\nCₓHᵧ + (4x + y)/4 O₂ → xCO₂ + (y/2)H₂O\n\nWater: y/2 = 1, so y = 2\nOxygen: (4x + 2)/4 = 2.5, so 4x + 2 = 10 and x = 2\n\nFormula = C₂H₂, so the hydrocarbon is ethyne.\nCheck: C₂H₂ + 2½O₂ → 2CO₂ + H₂O; 1.5 × 10⁻³ mol needs 1.5 × 10⁻³ × 2.5 × 22 400 = 84 cm³ O₂ and gives 1.5 × 10⁻³ × 18 000 = 27 mg H₂O.",
