    "model": "Le Chatelier's principle: when a system at equilibrium is disturbed (a change is made to the conditions), the position of equilibrium shifts so as to oppose (minimise) the change.\n(i) Adding concentrated HCl increases the chloride ion concentration. The equilibrium shifts to the left to use up (remove) the extra Cl⁻, so more blue CoCl₄²⁻ forms and the solution turns more blue.\n(ii) The forward reaction is exothermic, so the reverse reaction is endothermic. Raising the temperature favours the endothermic (reverse) reaction, which absorbs the added heat, so the equilibrium shifts to the left. The mixture changes colour from pink towards blue (becomes more blue).",
    "model_source": "audited",
    "answerStatus": "written",
