    "model": "Method 1 (vaporising in a flask)\nDiagram: a conical flask sealed with foil with a small pinhole in it, clamped in a beaker of boiling water so that at least half of the flask is under the water; a thermometer is in the water. Labels: flask with foil/pinhole, water bath, thermometer.\nMass: weigh the flask and foil. Add a little of the liquid, cover with the foil and heat in the water bath until all the liquid has vaporised (flask appears empty). Cool, dry and reweigh. The mass of sample is the difference between the two masses.\nVolume: fill the flask with water and empty it into a graduated cylinder; this gives the volume of the vapour.\nTemperature: read the temperature of the water bath with the thermometer (not assumed to be 100 °C).\n\nMethod 2 (gas syringe)\nDiagram: a gas syringe with a self-sealing (septum) cap, surrounded by a heating device (oven, steam jacket or beaker of hot water) with a thermometer. Labels: gas syringe, self-sealing cap, heating device.\nMass: weigh a hypodermic syringe containing the liquid, inject some into the gas syringe, reweigh the hypodermic syringe; the mass of sample is the difference.\nVolume: read the volume of vapour directly from the gas syringe.\nTemperature: read the thermometer in the heating device.",
