    "model": "Electronegativity: a measure of the relative attraction an atom has for the shared electrons in a covalent bond.\n\n(i) Bonding in water (dot and cross): oxygen has 6 outer electrons (dots), each hydrogen has 1 (cross).\n      ••\n  H ×• O •× H\n      ••\nEach O–H bond is a shared pair (one cross from H, one dot from O), so there are two bonding pairs. Oxygen also has two lone pairs (4 non-bonding dots), giving it 8 outer electrons and each hydrogen 2.\n\n(ii) Shape: v-shaped (bent).\nAngle: 104°.\nExplanation: the two lone pairs on oxygen repel more strongly than bonding pairs (lone pair–lone pair > lone pair–bond pair > bond pair–bond pair). This pushes the two O–H bonds closer together, reducing the bond angle below 109° to about 104°.",
