    "marks": 18,
    "model": "Mechanism (electrophilic addition of HCl to ethene):\nThe electron-rich double bond repels the approaching HCl, which is polarised (Hδ+–Clδ−) and approaches side-on.\nHCl splits by heterolytic fission into ions: HCl → H⁺ + Cl⁻\nH⁺ uses the pi electrons of the double bond to bond with one carbon atom.\nThe other carbon is left positively charged (a carbonium ion / carbocation, CH₃–CH₂⁺).\nCl⁻ approaches and attacks / bonds\nto the C⁺ (carbon atom carrying the positive charge), forming CH₃CH₂Cl.\nEvidence: Adding bromine water to ethene gives 2-bromoethanol (CH₂BrCH₂OH) as well as 1,2-dibromoethane. Alternatively, bromine water containing sodium chloride gives 1-bromo-2-chloroethane (CH₂BrCH₂Cl), showing the attacking ions add in two steps.",
