    "label": "(b)(ii)",
    "marks": 12,
    "model": "Paper chromatography method:\nMedium (stationary phase): a strip of chromatography paper with a pencil base line about 2 cm from the bottom.\nApplication of sample: place a small spot of the indicator mixture on the base line using a capillary tube and let it dry.\nElution (development): suspend the paper in a covered container holding a little solvent (mobile phase, e.g. ethanol/water) so the solvent level is below the spot. The solvent rises up the paper by capillary action, carrying the indicators with it.\nSeparation: the indicators move at different rates (different solubility in the solvent and attraction to the paper), so they separate into distinct coloured spots. Remove the paper when the solvent nears the top and mark the solvent front.\n\nLabelled diagram:\n\n   |  paper strip (stationary phase)  |\n   |  - - solvent front - -           |\n   |   o  separated spot 2            |\n   |   o  separated spot 1            |\n   |  ---x--- base line + original spot (above solvent level)\n   |__________________________________|\n   ~~~ solvent (mobile phase) ~~~  in covered container/beaker",
